enchilada: BT: btreal=4, bluetoothd's HCI relayed to the real WCN3990 (Broadcom identity and VSCs stay canned)

Round 2 of logs/bt/real-controller-plan.md. sweet_btq.c stage 4 (after stage 3): the ADT
local-mac-address goes to the chip with EDL_WRITE_BD_ADDR 0xfc14 (btqca.c qca_set_bdaddr, most
significant octet first; Read_BD_ADDR then says whether it matches, never printing it), then the
bring-up thread becomes the proxy's RX side: it polls SE6's RX FIFO into a 4 KiB buffer, taking
words only while there is room (else RFR holds the chip off), splits H4 events / ACL / SCO (at most
5 + 1024 bytes; longer headers resync byte by byte) and hands each packet to sweet_bt_from_chip();
every in-band-sleep WAKE_IND gets its WAKE_ACK (the host never sends SLEEP_IND, so hci_qca.c's
host TX side stays awake and never needs WAKE_IND). sweet_btq_send() writes one packet under the
SE lock; a TX failure ends the proxy. sweet_bt.c: the first non-BlueTool unit that connects once
the proxy is up owns the chip; its commands go to the chip except Read_Local_Version (fake
Broadcom BCM4357B1), Read_BD_ADDR (ADT) and every OGF 0x3f VSC (canned, 0273/0274 kept), its
ACL/SCO/ISO packets are relayed whole (no longer dropped), and the chip's packets reach only that
unit, Qualcomm vendor events (0xff) dropped and counted. BlueTool's sessions stay canned: the
Broadcom .hcd never reaches the chip. Relayed packets log 6 bytes (peer addresses). btreal<=3
behave as before.

